{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The outcomes of the two lotteries are: Lottery A has outcomes of -1000 and 0 (each with 50% probability), and Lottery B has a single outcome of -500 with 100% probability. To compute the sum of all pairwise differences of outcomes between the lotteries, we compare each outcome in A with each in B. Since B has one outcome (-500) and A has two (-1000 and 0), there are two pairwise differences: (-1000) - (-500) = -500, and 0 - (-500) = +500. The sum of these differences is (-500) + (+500) = 0. The sum of outcome differences is therefore neutral (0), meaning the reason would be indifferent between A and B, as neither has a more favorable sum. The relevant information for the reason — the pairwise outcome differences and their sum — can be fully derived from the problem.",
